    "notes": "Two sessions: 27 April 1970 (tracks 1, 2, 4, 6, 8) and 13 May 1970 (tracks 3, 5, 7, 9), at National Recording Studios, New York City. DATE CONFLICT PRESERVED: Wikipedia, the AllMusic album page and the LP listing give 13 May; Ellingtonia's discography gives 11 May 1970 for the second session — unresolved, both recorded here as 13 May (obs) with the alternative noted. Mixed, edited and sequenced at Atlantic Recording Studios, New York (AllMusic: Ilhan Mimaroglu, editing/mixing/sequencing) — that is post-production, not a recording site. Producer credit from AllMusic; engineer Roger Rhodes. Wikipedia's personnel list omits Mercer Ellington, who appears on Ellingtonia's second-session roster and in the AllMusic credits. Wild Bill Davis plays organ on track 1 only. Johnny Hodges died between the sessions; these are his last recordings. track_assignments_complete is false because the per-track solo/chair detail beyond session membership is not fully documented. PLACE: National Recording Studios is not in the canonical place set, so it is minted here complete — 730 Fifth Avenue, New York (documented in the 1979 Record World studio directory and Wikipedia's National Video Center article; National occupied that address from 1959 until moving to W. 42nd Street in 1981, so it is the right room for 1970). Coordinates come from the Crown Building entity at 730 Fifth Avenue, not from OSM's match for the bare street address, which resolved to upper Manhattan off-target; the address itself is street-level `obs`. STAGED STATE (2026-10-20): the 27 April session was attached to national-recording-studios; the 13 May entry staged with no place because the site_new object appeared only on the first recording_sites entry and stage-candidate resolves each entry independently. Repair means writing session.studio_id, which is outside the edit whitelist — flagged to John rather than written here."
  },
  "ballot": {
    "album": "Duke Ellington — New Orleans Suite (1970)",
    "scope_check": {
      "in_window": true,
      "boundary_note": "No hard scope boundary applies; the stylistic question is whether this backward-looking orchestral suite is best described as post-bop or as Ellington's distinct modern big-band continuum."
    },
    "tier": "consensus_core",
